# Gitleaks findings waived BY FINGERPRINT, one line each, with the reason.
#
# THE RULE THIS FILE OBEYS, taken from security.yml's own header: never re-mute
# a scanner to turn a red build green. The precedent it names is pip-audit —
# `--ignore-vuln <ID> with a comment saying why`, one CVE at a time, never the
# whole tool. A fingerprint here is that same shape: it silences exactly one
# occurrence in one commit and leaves every future finding loud, including a
# second occurrence of the same rule in the same file.
#
# Format: <commit>:<path>:<rule-id>:<line>

# A TEST FIXTURE THAT NEVER HELD A CREDENTIAL.
#
# `backend/tests/test_credentials_are_stripped.py` needs a value that survives a
# round trip unchanged, to prove a clean credential is not mangled by the
# whitespace stripper. The value chosen was a sixteen-character lowercase-hex
# run — the exact shape `generic-api-key` exists to catch — so the scanner was
# right to stop on it, and the file now uses an unmistakably fake string
# instead (`_CLEAN_FIXTURE_VALUE`).
#
# The fix cannot reach BACKWARDS, though: gitleaks runs with `fetch-depth: 0`
# and scans history, and the commit below still contains the old literal. That
# commit is on this branch only — never on `main`, and this PR squash-merges, so
# `main` never receives it. Rewriting the branch's history to erase four
# characters would be a far larger and riskier act than recording why one dead
# line is exempt.
#
# Deliberately NOT an allowlist rule or a path exclusion: either would blind the
# scanner to the next REAL key in this file. This waives one line of one commit.
e495463b43859d3678d3807dbbdf59be208e449e:backend/tests/test_credentials_are_stripped.py:generic-api-key:101

